# Code navigation: no single tool's reference list is complete

Always on: this governs ordinary working sessions in any language, so it cannot
be scoped to a file glob. Tool choice, index coverage and the measured
comparison are in the `reference-coverage` skill. One thing has to be known
before that skill is reached, because it is what makes the skill worth invoking:

**Neither codegraph nor a language server is complete on "who references this",
and both fail silently.** A language server inherits every exclusion in the
project's tsconfig, so a repo excluding tests answers without them; it cannot
see across packages in a monorepo, and once found 1 of 5 referencing files while
reporting nothing wrong. codegraph merges same-named symbols across packages and
attributes callers to the wrong one. A partial answer is indistinguishable from
a complete one.

So before a rename, a deletion, a signature change, or any edit whose
correctness depends on having found every caller, invoke `reference-coverage`
and confirm the list against a second independent source. Treat any single
tool's answer as a lower bound. A false positive costs one look; a false
negative ships a broken build.

State which tool produced a claim when it matters: "codegraph reports 5 callers,
grep confirms" is checkable, "there are 5 callers" is not.
